<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apepi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1590–1550 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Apophis (pharaoh)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apepi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> king of the Fifteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> in the eastern Delta for some forty years in the 16th century BCE, the best-attested ruler of his line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</a> was copied in the 33rd year of his reign. Late in his reign the Theban kings of the Seventeenth Dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seqenenre-tao">Seqenenre Tao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamose">Kamose</a>, went to war against him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apepi.jpg" alt="Apepi" width="228" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keith Schengili-Roberts · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ScarabBearingNameOfApophis_MuseumOfFineArtsBoston.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
